1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,460
Sorting orders a collection. Streams transform how you process one.

2
00:00:04,460 --> 00:00:08,187
Instead of nested loops everywhere — declare a pipeline.

3
00:00:08,187 --> 00:00:11,648
Source, intermediate operations, terminal operation.

4
00:00:11,648 --> 00:00:15,709
Lazy until it needs to run. Expressive when the problem fits.

5
00:00:15,709 --> 00:00:19,103
Today — the mental model that makes Streams useful.

6
00:00:19,103 --> 00:00:22,498
Data as a pipeline — not a pile of index variables.

7
00:00:22,498 --> 00:00:24,001
Episode Twenty-Six.

8
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:27,404
Streams introduction — pipelines over data.

9
00:00:27,404 --> 00:00:29,926
A Stream is not a new collection type.

10
00:00:29,926 --> 00:00:33,974
It is a sequence of elements supporting aggregate operations.

11
00:00:33,974 --> 00:00:38,619
You build a pipeline — then a terminal operation triggers computation.

12
00:00:38,619 --> 00:00:41,075
Streams are single-use. Consume once.

13
00:00:41,075 --> 00:00:45,587
They can come from collections, arrays, generators, or I/O wrappers.

14
00:00:45,587 --> 00:00:47,910
Think recipe first — result second.

15
00:00:47,910 --> 00:00:50,367
Operations split into two families.

16
00:00:50,367 --> 00:00:55,421
Intermediate — filter, map, flatMap, sorted, distinct — return a stream.

17
00:00:55,421 --> 00:01:01,529
Terminal — collect, reduce, forEach, count, anyMatch — produce a result or side effect.

18
00:01:01,529 --> 00:01:05,249
Without a terminal operation, nothing useful happens.

19
00:01:05,249 --> 00:01:08,900
Keep intermediate steps free of surprising mutation.

20
00:01:08,900 --> 00:01:11,848
Readable pipelines beat clever one-liners.

21
00:01:11,848 --> 00:01:14,285
Laziness is the key performance idea.

22
00:01:14,285 --> 00:01:18,500
Intermediate operations record what to do — they do not run yet.

23
00:01:18,500 --> 00:01:21,991
A terminal operation pulls data through the pipeline.

24
00:01:21,991 --> 00:01:25,021
anyMatch can stop early. limit can bound work.

25
00:01:25,021 --> 00:01:28,907
That is why filter then findFirst can skip unused elements.

26
00:01:28,907 --> 00:01:33,386
Use laziness — do not fight it with eager side effects mid-pipeline.

27
00:01:33,386 --> 00:01:35,847
collect is how most pipelines finish.

28
00:01:35,847 --> 00:01:39,039
Collectors.toList and toSet materialize results.

29
00:01:39,039 --> 00:01:42,430
toMap builds maps carefully — watch duplicate keys.

30
00:01:42,430 --> 00:01:45,423
groupingBy clusters elements by a classifier.

31
00:01:45,423 --> 00:01:49,280
joining builds strings without manual StringBuilder noise.

32
00:01:49,280 --> 00:01:53,004
Choose a collector that matches the shape you need next.

33
00:01:53,004 --> 00:01:54,253
When Streams shine.

34
00:01:54,253 --> 00:01:57,805
Transform and filter chains that would be noisy loops.

35
00:01:57,805 --> 00:02:00,633
Pipelines that read like the business rule.

36
00:02:00,633 --> 00:02:03,723
Optional parallelism later — after you measure.

37
00:02:03,723 --> 00:02:08,195
When not — heavy mutable accumulation that is clearer as a for-loop.

38
00:02:08,195 --> 00:02:11,878
Clarity first. Streams are a tool, not a purity contest.

39
00:02:11,878 --> 00:02:13,296
Three common mistakes.

40
00:02:13,296 --> 00:02:16,197
One — reusing a stream after it was consumed.

41
00:02:16,197 --> 00:02:21,161
Two — sneaking side effects into map instead of keeping transformations pure.

42
00:02:21,161 --> 00:02:24,643
Three — sprinkling parallel without evidence it helps.

43
00:02:24,643 --> 00:02:28,253
Also — giant pipelines nobody can debug at a breakpoint.

44
00:02:28,253 --> 00:02:30,896
Good stream code is still boringly clear.

45
00:02:30,896 --> 00:02:35,214
Interview question — what is a Stream, and why does laziness matter?

46
00:02:35,214 --> 00:02:39,469
A pipeline over elements with intermediate and terminal operations.

47
00:02:39,469 --> 00:02:44,295
Laziness delays work until a terminal operation — enabling short-circuiting.

48
00:02:44,295 --> 00:02:47,914
Mention collect as the common way to materialize results.

49
00:02:47,914 --> 00:02:52,296
Contrast with collections — streams do not store elements themselves.

50
00:02:52,296 --> 00:02:55,026
That answer shows conceptual understanding.

51
00:02:55,026 --> 00:02:59,248
Pipelines are in place. Next — collectors with real shape.

52
00:02:59,248 --> 00:03:02,233
Episode Twenty-Seven — Stream Collectors.

53
00:03:02,233 --> 00:03:05,873
Grouping, partitioning, and downstream collectors.

54
00:03:05,873 --> 00:03:06,892
See you there.
